-- SHADOW-SEMANTICS cell D24 (S1-NS (b), RETURN-POSITION): this module DEFINES its own `zed`
-- and reaches `smod` only through `import smod.{sf}` -- which admits neither the type name
-- `Zed` nor the method name `zed`. So `zed` is not in S1's right operand, it is not a
-- shadow, and the module's own binding answers: 42 + 0 = 42.
--
-- 🚨 THE WHOLE CORPUS WAS ARG-POSITION UNTIL THIS CELL. Derive it: every interface method
-- in test/shadow_fixtures/ mentioned its typaram in an ARGUMENT. `zed : a` does not -- the
-- typaram appears only in the RESULT -- and that single axis selects a completely different
-- input to the mark pass: `returnPosMethodNames`, which was concatenated RAW while the
-- arg-position inputs were filtered. Dispatch-eligibility was therefore a strict SUPERSET of
-- shadow-hood, which is S1-NS's own "Newly NON-CONFORMANT (1)", and 52 assertions were green
-- over it because not one of them could express a return-position method.
--
-- Measured before the fix: `run` printed 777 (the impl body, through an interface this
-- module cannot name) while the BUILT BINARY printed 42 -- both exit 0, no diagnostic on any
-- verb. On emit the occurrence is mangled to `main__zed`, so the bare-keyed `rpNames` misses
-- it and the filtered `shadowMap` decides; on `run` the name is still bare and `rpNames`
-- matches. That is why this cell must be graded on `run` AND the built binary: an
-- ACCEPT/REJECT column sees nothing, and either verb alone sees nothing either.
import smod.{sf}

zed : Int
zed = 42

main = println (zed + sf 0)
